{
  "teamId": 115,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Colorado Rockies entered the season in full transition, trading away franchise cornerstones and embracing a youth movement that would define their summer. April was a brutal introduction to the new reality — a 9-17 record punctuated by a bullpen that couldn't hold leads and opposing hitters feasting on Colorado's relief corps. Daniel Bard, in particular, was victimized repeatedly in high-leverage moments, surrendering pivotal home runs that turned winnable games into losses. The early weeks set an uncomfortable tone: this was a team capable of flashing brilliance but deeply prone to unraveling at the worst moments.\n\nMay brought more of the same inconsistency, though glimmers of something genuine began to emerge. Charlie Blackmon delivered the most electric moment of the early season with a go-ahead three-run blast, and Josh Fuentes proved to be a surprise contributor with clutch extra-base hits. The team went 11-17 in May, still clearly below the playoff line, but the offense was starting to show teeth. Trevor Story was quietly playing his final months in a Rockies uniform, and the franchise was beginning to understand it was watching the end of an era rather than the beginning of one.\n\nJune represented the genuine turning point — not because the Rockies caught fire, but because they started winning close games instead of losing them. A 14-13 record in June signaled that the roster, patchwork as it was, was learning to compete. July and August became the heart of the season's identity: a .500-ish grind where Connor Joe, Elias Díaz, and Ryan McMahon took turns delivering clutch long balls. Díaz in particular transformed into an unexpected star, belting 14 home runs and repeatedly coming through with runners on base. C.J. Cron quietly crossed 20 home runs, giving the lineup a genuine power threat in the middle.\n\nBy September the Rockies had already ceded any playoff ambitions, but they continued to play with competitiveness, posting a 13-13 record and showcasing what a rebuilt roster might look like. Ryan McMahon's dramatic home run on September 9th — worth nearly 0.76 WPA — was the season's loudest offensive moment, a reminder of the talent still on the roster. The 74-87 final record was not a surprise, but the second half suggested a franchise slowly assembling the pieces for something better. The Rockies finished as a team that rarely embarrassed itself, even if it never quite threatened anyone either.",
  "chapters": [
